WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 13 Leader Video
Unit 4, Lesson 1: Morality

1
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.940
Alright, so this week we're talking about morality.

2
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.580
Now, it might seem on the surface that to talk about morality is pretty

3
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.400
straightforward, right? It's right versus wrong.

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.220
It's what we should be doing, what we shouldn't be doing, and we're really comparing

5
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.100
that all to God as the standard.

6
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:29.680
So it seems pretty straightforward it seems pretty simple I mean but I want you to

7
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.440
take a second and step back because it may not be as simple as you

8
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.740
think or as you hope because I want you to think for a minute about really what

9
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:45.120
we're asking students to do we're asking them to differentiate

10
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.900
between right and wrong and we're asking them to do that when

11
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:55.920
they're taught that some of these things that we're talking about are just a little

12
00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.660
bit more than mere opinion. See, students are taught on a whole different

13
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:05.080
level than maybe what we're used to, so you're going to experience something that

14
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.340
maybe you don't realize you're going to experience.

15
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:13.420
When we stand on stage as leaders and we're talking about Jesus and we're talking

16
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.480
about truth and we're talking about these fundamental facts about the Christian

17
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.900
faith and we're telling them what they ought to be doing what they ought not to be

18
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.480
doing. Students can often be looking at us as we're thinking we're talking about

19
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:29.040
fact and we're thinking we're talking pretty black and white kind of things and that

20
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.100
may not necessarily be the case when some of our students are looking up to us

21
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.900
and thinking well that's your opinion.

22
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:43.420
That's what you think and I'm not sure if I actually want to agree with

23
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:49.280
your opinion so this is a good week it's a good time

24
00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.220
to take a step back make sure you meet with your small group leaders and make

25
00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:00.640
sure you talk through a little bit about what they might experience in their small

26
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:06.060
groups and talk a little bit about some feedback that you can get from the students

27
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.760
about some of the things that you're talking about from the main stage about

28
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.700
what their thoughts are on right versus wrong and what they're being taught in

29
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.980
school and how they feel about certain topics.

30
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:23.000
You might be comforted, but you also might be surprised and you might get some

31
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.840
answers that you're not real thrilled with, but it's a good starting point and

32
00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:30.000
something that you can continue to work through.

33
00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.460
So I'll never forget back when I was teaching, I'd walk in in the morning and you'd

34
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.900
be stepping through the hallways and you have to literally step over all of the

35
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.580
students that were sitting in the hallways and talking, all these great

36
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.340
conversations and activity going on in the hallways.

37
00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.840
It was a great sort of experience in the morning to see some students smiling faces

38
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.820
and other students not so smiling faces, and it was awesome.

39
00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:56.120
And one day at a teacher's meeting, we got talking about some of the students, and

40
00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:01.060
we realized something that most of us just were completely blind to.

41
00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.520
The students were doing more than just sitting in the hallways hanging out.

42
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.980
Well, the students were sitting in the hallways copying each other's homework for

43
00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.980
their first class or their first couple of classes throughout the day.

44
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.840
And some of us talked about it and said well are they collaborating and working

45
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.440
together on something? Or are they just straight up copying their homework which

46
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.660
would be obviously wrong.

47
00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.260
And so we talked about it, we debated it a little bit, and then we decided let's

48
00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.560
confront some of our students. Let's talk through with some of our students.

49
00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.960
Maybe they just don't realize what they're doing which would seem strange but maybe

50
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:36.380
they don't realize what they're doing is wrong.

51
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.420
So we pulled a few students aside with no intention of getting them in trouble and

52
00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.980
just said, hey, what are you doing in the hallways in the morning?

53
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.520
And with no regard for possibly getting in trouble or having us to call

54
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.360
our parents or some kind of consequence coming down on them, they just were like,

55
00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.140
yeah, we're just copying homework.

56
00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.060
That's what we're doing. No big deal.

57
00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.060
And so we asked them, well, do you think this is wrong?

58
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.480
And the response was simple. Well, no.

59
00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:10.160
Well, why not? Well, because we just don't.

60
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.080
That was their answer. They just don't.

61
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.700
And if you do a little bit of research into the world of education and what students

62
00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:23.580
are being taught is right and wrong, it's actually becoming more and more closer to

63
00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:28.260
what we consider to be opinion. And students are being taught that anytime we use

64
00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.880
words like feeling or what they believe.

65
00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:36.820
It's more about what they think is simply an opinion.

66
00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.340
Well, this is sort of causing some problems, right?

67
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:45.140
Because now when we start talking about things more than just copying homework as

68
00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.560
being an opinion, we say things like, well, are all men or all people created

69
00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.620
equally? Well, is that a fact or is that an opinion?

70
00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:59.420
What about uh it's wrong in our country to drink under the age of 21

71
00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:05.400
is that a fact or is that just an opinion of somebody what

72
00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:11.080
about killing another unjustly something we all consider at least hope all

73
00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:16.480
consider to be a universal truth that killing another unjustly as in murder is

74
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:22.080
wrong, then is that fact or is that

75
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.060
opinion? So the slippery slope that the students are starting down is these things

76
00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.120
aren't necessarily becoming a little bit more based on opinion.

77
00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:36.080
So then when we get into things like same-sex marriage, fact or

78
00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.460
opinion. So as you stand on a main stage or you stand in front of a group of

79
00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:46.380
students and you start talking about what Scripture says is fact.

80
00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.960
They are being taught that these things are often just opinion.

81
00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:59.760
So talking about morality is vitally, vitally important in terms of helping

82
00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:05.120
them establish what our moral compass actually is and where we get our morals from.

83
00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.520
There's a little story I like to tell when it comes to morality.

84
00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:12.920
It's a story about little Johnny.

85
00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.120
And little Johnny is the little first or second grader that gets an assignment from

86
00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.980
school to go home and measure how tall he is.

87
00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:25.120
And he fumbles around his house, and he's trying to figure out how to measure

88
00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:28.640
himself so he can go back to school and be a huge success and get a great grade and

89
00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:31.540
all those wonderful things. And he scrambles all over the house, and he can't figure

90
00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:36.920
out anything. And finally, he has this massively beautifully wonderful idea, and he

91
00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:40.900
runs to school the next day, and all the little boys and girls are going up to the

92
00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.400
front the class and some say you know I'm this tall according to my dad and I'm this

93
00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.120
many basketballs high and you know all these other different objects and johnny gets

94
00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:53.160
up in front of the classroom and big smile beaming says I am one Johnny tall

95
00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.860
and the teacher you know kind of looks at him and sort of reacts and johnny

96
00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:04.640
didn't pass the assignment because johnny has we have no idea how tall

97
00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:10.020
johnny really is because johnny will never be more or less than one johnny tall no

98
00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.880
matter how big Johnny actually gets in the future.

99
00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:18.040
See, we can't use ourselves to measure ourselves.

100
00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.740
We need something beyond us, something external to us, and morality functions the

101
00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:28.780
same way. And what's beautiful about morality, as much as it's difficult to follow

102
00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:34.020
what God desires for us to do, the fact that we have morals, the fact that we

103
00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:39.560
understand right from wrong helps us see who God is, points us to God, gives us

104
00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:44.300
evidence for God, because we need something beyond ourselves to understand what the

105
00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:46.560
difference between right and wrong is.

106
00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.960
So what can we do, right? So there's a lot of cautionary there.

107
00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:54.780
So what can you actually do? Well, the first thing that you can do is approach all

108
00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.860
of this, first and foremost, with grace and with love.

109
00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.800
Remember, most of your students, I hope all of your students really, are just trying

110
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:07.580
to love their friends and love people, and be tolerant, and get along, and live

111
00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.240
peacefully among everybody else.

112
00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:12.640
I'm sure that's the case with almost all, if not all of your students.

113
00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:15.720
So they're not trying to be malicious.

114
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:17.920
They're not trying to necessarily defy authority.

115
00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.760
So approach them with a tremendous amount of grace and a tremendous amount of love

116
00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:26.140
and listen. Listen to the reasons that they're giving.

117
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.080
That doesn't mean don't challenge those reasons, but make sure that you thoroughly,

118
00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.740
thoroughly listen to, you know, why they're saying what they're saying.

119
00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.140
Secondly, know where they're coming from.

120
00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:40.380
Talk to them about where they're hearing their ideas.

121
00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.460
You know, which classes even? Which teachers?

122
00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:47.240
Is it at home? Is it online? Is it TV and media?

123
00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.360
Where are they getting these ideas from?

124
00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.400
Now that doesn't mean you don't want to tell them all your ideas are stupid.

125
00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.700
You just want to hear where they're from and hear again why they believe those

126
00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:01.900
things and why do you think those things are true and help them work through what

127
00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:05.940
those thoughts actually are. No different than helping us, you know, you guys

128
00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:10.040
working through all of these topics and answering great questions and asking more

129
00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:12.280
great questions and trying to figure things out.

130
00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:16.060
You want to help students navigate this a little bit rather than just shoot them

131
00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:20.260
down. And finally, don't be afraid to challenge their thinking.

132
00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:23.340
Some students might come at you a little bit.

133
00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:25.360
Some students might get a little bit aggressive.

134
00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.820
Some might just shut down inside themselves if they feel like their opinion is not

135
00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:32.100
valued or not being heard.

136
00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:38.000
That's okay as long as you draw that back out of them and don't get offended if a

137
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.080
student kind of comes at you.

138
00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:45.460
Challenge their thinking, but do so again in love and in understanding and by

139
00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:48.160
listening and taking the time necessary.

140
00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.280
And make sure that this is communicated to your small group leaders because in that

141
00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:56.800
context, in that community, in the community that you are trying to build within

142
00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.380
your youth groups, it is so, so important that those students feel like they can

143
00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:07.360
open up and they feel like they can offer that opinion and be accepted but also

144
00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:08.300
be challenged.

